# Line-ending policy. This file is not cosmetic — two entries below are load-bearing.

# Default: let git normalise text in the repository, check out native in the worktree.
* text=auto

# Patches must NEVER be converted. `git apply` compares context byte for byte, and a
# patch checked out with CRLF fails to apply against an LF tree (and vice versa).
# Marking them -text disables all conversion in both directions.
*.patch -text
*.diff  -text

# Shell scripts must be LF. A CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter
# literally named "bash\r" and the script dies with "bad interpreter".
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Windows batch files must be CRLF, or cmd.exe mis-parses labels and multi-line blocks.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Arma / Enfusion sources. Treated as text so diffs are readable, forced to LF so the
# same file does not churn between a Windows Workbench and a Linux server checkout.
*.c     text eol=lf
*.et    text eol=lf
*.conf  text eol=lf
*.gproj text eol=lf
*.layout text eol=lf
*.meta  text eol=lf
*.st    text eol=lf

# Binary assets: no conversion, no diffing.
*.edds  binary
*.xob   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.wav   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.pak   binary
*.rdb   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
